{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["The reason for <b>And Korah assembled against them all the congregation</b> — he assembled the great men of the people who would regularly gather at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, or the firstborn of all Israel, who were fit for the divine service. And Scripture did not mention \"the people\" as in the case of the calf and the spies, for if it had been all Israel, He would have said, \"I will consume them in a moment and make of you a great nation.\" And all that I have mentioned concerning the firstborn (above, verses 1, 5) follows the way of our Sages (Zevachim 112a), who say that the divine service belonged to the firstborn. But according to the plain sense, all Israel were initially fit for the service of the sacrifices, for so is the law always regarding private altars, and Aaron was chosen for the service of the Tabernacle and the Temple, and Korah was contesting this choice and wanted to restore the services to all Israel, for all the congregation, every one of them, is holy:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The quotation 'I will consume them in a moment and make of you a great nation' alludes to the divine responses in the golden calf and spy narratives; Ramban cites the sense rather than the exact wording of any single verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:13.721511865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}